    "result_text": "I've added the summary panel to the home screen, and the summary test passes when run with `bin/e2e`.\n\nThis was a front-end-only change: the data the panel needs was already on the screen, so there's no new endpoint, stored data or audit record. Every figure is worked out by the same functions the lists already use, so it agrees with them:\n\n- **Per grade (A, B, C):** the number of people on that grade today, leaving out anyone with a retirement date, and their due-totals added up. A person whose due-total shows `n/a` counts as 0 here, the same way the overall due-total treats them.\n- **Per period:** the same due-total the period list shows, including the figure fixed when a period was closed.\n- **Per status:** the number of claims in each status, across every claim on the sheet. That includes claims of retired people, whose rows are hidden from the people list; the test expects them to be counted.\n\nThree things you might not expect:\n- **`PENDING` always shows 0.** The test expects a count for it, but the app has no such claim status, so no claim can ever reach it. I didn't add the status.\n- **Future retirement dates count as retired.** The grade counts leave out anyone with a retirement date, even one after today, because that's how the people list decides who is hidden. One mismatch remains: someone retired who still has a draft claim stays in the list but isn't counted in the summary.\n- **Moved helper:** I moved the hook that works out a period's due-total out of `features/periods/periods.slot.tsx` into `features/periods/periods.ts` so the summary can share it. The periods list still uses it unchanged.\n\nThe panel itself is in `features/summary/summary.slot.tsx`.",
